const modelCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · The physics core', note: 'General circulation models (GCMs) solve conservation of energy, mass and momentum on a rotating sphere — the weather-forecast lesson\'s equations, run for centuries instead of days, with the ocean, ice, land and carbon cycle coupled in.' },
    { math: '2 · Resolution vs complexity', note: 'Global models divide Earth into ~100-km cells; regional models nest down to ~10 km for local detail. Processes smaller than the grid (clouds, convection) are "parameterised" — the main source of model disagreement, and of honest uncertainty bands.' },
    { math: '3 · Scenarios, not predictions', note: ' SSPs (Shared Socioeconomic Pathways): coherent futures for population, economy, policy — from SSP1-1.9 (net zero ~2050, ~1.5 °C) to SSP5-8.5 (unrestrained emissions). The model answers "IF this, THEN that"; humans choose the IF.' },
    { math: '4 · How we know models work', note: 'Hindcasts (reproducing the 20th century), paleoclimate tests (ice ages — the lesson), volcano responses (Pinatubo\'s cooling was predicted), and ensembles (many models, spread = uncertainty). The skill is real and measured: ~0.1 °C precision on 20-year global means.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/environmental-science/advanced/climate-modelling-and-scenarios">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Climate models are <strong>the most physically-constrained simulations ever built</strong> —
    the same fluid dynamics that forecasts weather, coupled to oceans, ice and carbon, run
    forward for centuries under scenarios humans choose. They are tested against the past
    continuously, and their uncertainty bands are honest work, not hedging.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the physics, the grids, the scenarios, the validation" widget="equationStep" config={modelCfg} />

  <h2>Reading projections like a professional</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Equilibrium climate sensitivity (ECS):</strong> warming per doubling of CO₂ — pinned by IPCC AR6 to 2.5–4 °C (narrowed from earlier ranges): the number that decides how urgent every pathway is.</li>
    <li><strong>Uncertainty is mostly scenario + feedback, not physics:</strong> temperature ranges in 2100 come mainly from unknown human choices, secondarily from cloud feedbacks — the physics of the direct CO₂ effect has been settled since Arrhenius (1896).</li>
    <li><strong>What models miss:</strong> ice-sheet dynamics (they under-predicted recent Antarctic loss — processes smaller than the grid), and tipping elements (handled as add-ons, the next lesson\'s subject). Model families are updated, not invalidated, by each surprise.</li>
    <li><strong>Attribution science runs the models backwards:</strong> comparing worlds with and without human emissions — the basis for "this heatwave was X× more likely" (the weather lesson\'s World Weather Attribution) and increasingly for climate litigation evidence.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> IPCC AR6 WG1 (2021); Arrhenius (1896); CMIP6 ensemble documentation; Hausfather, "Explainer: How climate models work" (Carbon Brief).</p>
</TopicPage>
